WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.680
One of the most powerful beings in the Marvel
Cinematic Universe is on her way to the big

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:05.180
screen.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:09.940
If you're unfamiliar with the latest MCU headliner
— Carol "Captain Marvel" Danvers — here

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:13.620
are a few basic facts you're going to want
to keep in mind as you settle in with your

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:14.120
popcorn.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.640
Captain Marvel tells the story of Carol Danvers,
played by Oscar-winner Brie Larson.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:23.760
Unlike most MCU flicks, the movie isn't set
in the present day, but instead takes place

00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:24.949
in the '90s.

00:00:24.949 --> 00:00:29.470
This gives Marvel plenty of time to show Danvers
becoming Captain Marvel and explain why she

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:34.840
was nowhere to be found as the events of 2008's
Iron Man ushered in the MCU superhero age,

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:40.140
and why she wasn't even referenced until the
post-credits scene in 2018's Infinity War.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.920
Danvers was first introduced as a character
in 1968's Marvel Super-Heroes #13.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:50.860
She was a retired Air Force pilot serving
a supporting role for the character Mar-Vell,

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:54.680
who had been created a year earlier and was
then occupying the role of Captain Marvel.

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:59.280
Cutting out decades of complicated comic book
backstory, the MCU seems to have decided to

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:03.490
streamline Danvers' origins — as they've
done more than once for the screen versions

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:05.140
of their classic characters in the past.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:10.260
One of the central forces in Captain Marvel
is a group that entered the Marvel Comics

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:14.620
Universe way back in 1967 in Fantastic Four
No. 65.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:19.220
We're talking about the Kree, a technologically
advanced race of aliens that generally take

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:23.180
the form of blue-skinned and purple-eyed beings
with otherwise human features.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.600
They aren't necessarily bad guys, but they
tend to have a bit of a mixed reputation,

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.240
as they can be militaristic and are infamous
for their constant squabbles around the galaxy.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:37.580
Danvers herself became a human/Kree hybrid
due to an accident that altered her DNA.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:39.200
"And you're a Kree?

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.080
A race of noble warriors?"

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:42.640
"Heroes.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:44.600
Noble warrior heroes."

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:49.340
But the movie actually won't be the Kree's
first foray into the MCU.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:54.140
The Ravager Yondu from Guardians of the Galaxy,
for example, revealed that he was sold to

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:55.160
the Kree in his youth.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:02:00.460
Ronan the Accuser was also a rogue Kree general
that ignored the treaty between his people

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:04.000
and Nova Corps and attacked Xandar with the
help of Thanos.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.780
The blue-skinned aliens have also made a number
of appearances on ABC's Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:14.000
While the Kree have a bit of a mixed history,
their longtime enemies, the extraterrestrial

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:19.140
race known as the Skrulls, are far less complicated,
at least as far as humans are concerned.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:23.220
Their appearances in the comics usually correlate
with Earth invasions or their more distant

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.340
— and ancient — quarrels with the Kree.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:30.670
The Skrulls first appeared in the comics in
Fantastic Four #2 in 1962, making them slightly

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:33.260
more well-established than Danvers or the
Kree.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:37.060
The most important thing you're going to want
to remember about the Skrulls is that they're

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:41.950
shapeshifters, able to take on whatever appearance
they need to in order to infiltrate and destroy

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:47.100
empires, organizations, and even herds of
superheroes, causing distress and distrust

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.060
from the inside out.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:53.560
This, of course, means they could already
be involved in more than one MCU movie, infiltrating

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:57.000
high positions and simply biding their time
for the perfect moment to strike.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.700
And you thought Hydra was sneaky!

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:00.940
"Hail Hydra."

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:06.940
Another element of Carol Danvers' Captain
Marvel comic book history is her ability to

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:12.480
take on her so-called Binary form, which first
appeared in The Uncanny X-Men #164.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:18.580
Danvers' ability to transform into the super-powered
Binary emerged when yet another evil alien

00:03:18.599 --> 00:03:24.200
race, known as the Brood, abducted and experimented
on her, further augmenting her already significantly

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:25.120
altered DNA.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:29.959
As Binary, Danvers could transform into an
energy-manipulating state that allowed her

00:03:29.959 --> 00:03:34.780
to lift more than 100 tons and fly at nearly
the speed of light — and these skills seem

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:36.400
to be in evidence in the trailers.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:41.280
In the third Captain Marvel trailer, we get
a quick glimpse of Carol suited up and leading

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:45.849
a group of aliens, all dressed in the same
uniform, walking in slow-mo through an other-worldly

00:03:45.849 --> 00:03:46.340
setting.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:51.000
It doesn't last long, but the view is most
likely our first look at Starforce, a team

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.580
of warriors that fight for the Kree.

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:57.330
The group was introduced in the comics in
Avengers #346 in 1992.

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:01.709
In the MCU, we've already met one Starforce
member — Korath, who appeared as one of

00:04:01.709 --> 00:04:04.740
Ronan the Accuser's lackeys in Guardians of
the Galaxy.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:06.620
Spoiler alert!

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:10.750
In an interview with Entertainment Tonight,
Samuel L. Jackson, who will be reprising his

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:15.010
role as Nick Fury in Captain Marvel, was asked
if he thought Danvers was worthy of being

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:17.960
the first female MCU character to get her
own movie.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:22.030
His response was, in essence, a breakdown
of all of her incredible powers, including

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:23.300
this little tidbit:

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:28.040
"[Captain Marvel is] one of the few people
in the Marvel universe that can time travel."

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:29.340
Hold the phone.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:30.500
She can what?

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:34.720
Time travel has been heavily rumored as one
of the main ways that the events of Infinity

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:39.600
War could be undone, but the context has usually
suggested that Ant-Man would somehow be using

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:42.580
the Quantum Realm or Doctor Strange would
be using the Time Stone.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:47.320
Needless to say, the quote caused an immediate
ripple effect of rumors and theories.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:51.700
The question that remains is how, exactly,
Danvers' mysterious time-traveling abilities

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:53.120
will be depicted in the film.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:58.600
Speaking of Nick Fury, the ex-director of
S.H.I.E.L.D. is clearly going to play an important

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:01.200
role in Carol Danvers' evolution into Captain
Marvel.

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:05.370
It's likely that the movie will double not
only as an origin story for Carol Danvers

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:08.400
but, in a secondary capacity, one for Nick
Fury as well.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:12.160
From showing his rise through the ranks of
S.H.I.E.L.D. to revealing why on Earth his

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:16.770
gut instinct after the finger snap would be
to page Captain Marvel, there are a lot of

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:19.940
Fury-based questions that could find their
answers in this one.

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:23.620
Oh, and he's also shown in the trailer with
two healthy eyes, so there's a good chance

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:26.740
we'll also see something regarding the origin
of that eyepatch.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:28.860
Finally, there's Goose.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:32.800
The fuzzy little guy isn't just tossed into
a scene for a bit of comic relief between

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:34.940
the future head of S.H.I.E.L.D. and a random
feline.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:38.540
It also happens to be Carol Danvers' pet…
and it's an alien.

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:43.310
Goose is a Flerken, an alien species that
typically resembles terrestrial cats, with

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:44.900
a couple of noticeable exceptions.

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:49.320
For example, they have a huge array of tentacles
that come out of their mouths, and they lay

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:50.520
eggs at a furious rate.

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:54.440
They also possess human-level intellect as
well as "pocket realities" of space and time

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:57.420
within their bodies — so yeah, not your
typical house cat.

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:01.630
In the comics, Danvers' pet was named Chewie,
but the name has apparently been changed from

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:06.740
one famous movie wingman to another for the
MCU, adding yet another nod to some hardcore

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:08.240
nostalgia in the process.

